    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.solve = dspy.ReAct(
            dspy.Signature(
                "db_id: str, question: str, evidence: str -> sql: str",
                "Produce a read-only SQLite query answering the question using the actual database. "
                "First inspect schema using query_database: SELECT name, sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'table' AND name NOT GLOB 'sqlite_*'. Never invent columns, joins, or category encodings. Issue only one SQL statement per tool call. "
                "Before drafting SQL, map each requested output, entity qualifier, condition, and evidence expression to the schema. Do not overlook category restrictions embedded in nouns such as food business or restaurant: an establishment table may contain many facility types, not just the requested kind. Inspect distinct facility/category values and relevant lookup tables to resolve that restriction; do not silently treat all establishments as the requested category. Use observed encodings and database relationships rather than guessing a category literal. If wording and stored categories differ, investigate with targeted probes before choosing the best-supported mapping. "
                "Choose the source of each measure by its meaning and grain, not merely by matching a column name. When both a master table and a detail/schedule table contain the same measure, inspect both. In publishing databases, royalty rates can be tiered in the royalty schedule (roysched), while titles.royalty is a title-level attribute. For titles having an above-average royalty rate, compare schedule-row royalty to the average over all royalty schedule rows, then join qualifying title IDs to titles for title, type, and price. A title qualifies if any schedule row exceeds that global average; do not replace this with a title-level royalty comparison or a per-title average. Preserve evidence denominators exactly: SUM(royalty) / COUNT(title_id) is not necessarily AVG(royalty) when values are nullable. Use real-valued division. "
                "Use evidence to identify intended columns, conditions, formulas, and aggregation grain. Translate nested arithmetic formulas faithfully, preserving parentheses and factors. Include all relevant partition tables with UNION ALL when records are split by region; use complete join keys to avoid multiplying rows. Verify ambiguous textual literals with targeted case-insensitive probes and use observed stored spellings. Inspect distinct status values before interpreting negative boolean states: NULL, empty text, 'false', and 0 are not interchangeable. Language names may be stored as codes; inspect the lookup. "
                "Plan output columns separately from filters and joins. Evidence mentioning an identifier does not make that identifier a requested output. Return only requested attributes in question order. When asked for explanations or distinct object classes of images satisfying coordinate conditions, select DISTINCT class labels alone, not image IDs. Coordinates constrain object instances; join the class lookup for labels. For a clinical observation's status, return description, value, and units in that order, even when description is constrained in WHERE. For a person's full identity with split name columns, return first, middle, and last name in schema order, retaining NULL middle names. For care plans return the requested description or start date, not unsolicited patient attributes. Otherwise avoid extra identifying or diagnostic columns. "
                "Respect aggregation grain. If evidence defines most as MAX of a stored games-played field, compare individual participation records with that maximum within the requested league; do not replace MAX with SUM across player records. For a singular who question selecting among players with maximum-game records and a college restriction, group qualifying records by full name and rank by count descending, selecting the top candidate. Derive maxima from data. For ordinary maximum questions retain ties unless a single winner or further ranking is requested. Count distinct entity IDs when joins duplicate entities unless evidence specifies a different counting grain. Averages over all ordered-book prices use order lines; averages over all works' dates use works before joining chapters. Use real-valued arithmetic for percentages. "
                "Before finalizing, audit every phrase in the question and evidence against the query: especially category restrictions, date ranges, source tables for repeated column names, average populations, and projected attributes. A query executing successfully is not proof of semantic correctness. Use bounded, targeted probes rather than retrieving entire large tables. Execute the actual final projected query before returning it, not merely a diagnostic query with a different projection. If final SQL changes after testing, test again. Repair errors using observed schema and error details. Investigate zero counts or empty results for mistaken literals or joins without removing legitimate conditions; verified empty results are acceptable. Truncated previews are not errors and must not introduce a LIMIT into final SQL. LIMIT is appropriate only for a requested top result. Return only the final SQL statement, without Markdown, commentary, result rows, or tool parameters."
            ),
            tools=[query_database],
            max_iters=20,
        )

    def forward(self, **inputs):
        result = self.solve(
            db_id=inputs['db_id'],
            question=inputs['question'],
            evidence=inputs['evidence'],
        )
        sql = str(result.sql).strip()
        if sql.startswith('```'):
            lines = sql.splitlines()
            if lines and lines[0].startswith('```'):
                lines = lines[1:]
            if lines and lines[-1].strip() == '```':
                lines = lines[:-1]
            sql = '\n'.join(lines).strip()
        return dspy.Prediction(sql=sql)
